{% section-card tone="intro" %}
## What is a data structure?

**A data structure is a deliberate arrangement of data that makes some operations cheap and accepts costs elsewhere.** An array makes indexed reads cheap because values are contiguous. A hash table spends extra memory to make key lookup fast. A balanced tree does more work per lookup so keys remain ordered.

The core invariant is simple: **choose for the operations and semantics the system must preserve, not for a structure's popularity.** Name the dominant reads and writes, decide whether answers must be exact or ordered, then check the cost at realistic scale.

{% callout variant="info" %}
Big O describes how work grows, not how fast a particular implementation runs. Allocation, cache locality, key distribution, load factor, and value size can decide the winner when two choices have the same asymptotic cost.
{% /callout %}
{% /section-card %}

{% section-card accent="blue" %}
## Choose from the dominant operation

Do not begin with a favorite structure. Begin with the operation consuming the latency or memory budget, then verify the choice against representative data.

{% process-flow %}
{% process-step number="1" title="Name the required operations" label="Workload" %}
List indexed reads, key lookups, inserts, deletes, ordered scans, membership tests, and relationship traversals with their expected frequency.
{% /process-step %}

{% process-step number="2" title="Preserve the needed semantics" label="Contract" %}
Decide whether answers must be exact, keys must stay ordered, priority must be visible, or relationships must be traversable.
{% /process-step %}

{% process-step number="3" title="Check time and memory bounds" label="Budget" %}
Compare expected and worst-case time, per-item overhead, allocation behavior, and cache locality at target scale.
{% /process-step %}

{% process-step number="4" title="Benchmark representative data" label="Evidence" %}
Measure the actual runtime and data distribution because constants, skew, and implementation details can dominate the theoretical model.
{% /process-step %}
{% /process-flow %}

{% interactive-block id="reference/data-structures-operation-selector" /%}
{% /section-card %}

{% section-card accent="violet" %}
## Read complexity as a growth model

Let `n` be the number of stored items, `m` the length of a key, `V` the number of graph vertices, and `E` the number of graph edges. These symbols identify what makes the work grow.

{% metric-strip %}
{% metric value="O(1)" label="Constant" detail="Work does not grow with n" tone="green" /%}
{% metric value="O(log n)" label="Logarithmic" detail="Doubling n adds about one step" tone="blue" /%}
{% metric value="O(n)" label="Linear" detail="Doubling n roughly doubles work" tone="amber" /%}
{% metric value="O(V + E)" label="Graph traversal" detail="Visit vertices and their edges" tone="violet" /%}
{% /metric-strip %}

- **Expected versus worst case:** Hash-table lookup is expected O(1), but adversarial collisions can make it O(n).
- **Amortized cost:** A dynamic-array append is usually O(1), with occasional O(n) growth spread across many appends.
- **Output-sensitive cost:** A tree range scan is O(log n + k), where `k` is the number of returned items.
- **Key-sensitive cost:** Trie work is O(m), so long keys matter even when the collection size does not.

{% callout variant="warn" %}
Never label every binary tree operation O(log n). An unbalanced binary search tree can become a chain and degrade to O(n). The logarithmic guarantee requires a balancing strategy such as an AVL or red-black tree.
{% /callout %}
{% /section-card %}

{% section-card accent="violet" %}
## Put time, memory, and semantics in the same budget

The fastest theoretical lookup may exceed the memory budget, while the smallest representation may weaken correctness. Use the model below to see why a production decision needs all three dimensions.

{% interactive-block id="reference/data-structures-tradeoff-lab" /%}
{% /section-card %}

{% section-card accent="green" %}
## Recognize the major structure families

The family tells you which relationship the representation preserves. The implementation determines the exact constants and operational behavior.

{% concept-grid columns="4" %}
{% concept-card title="Sequence" eyebrow="Position" icon="list" tone="blue" %}
Arrays, linked lists, stacks, and queues organize values by position or insertion order. They differ in locality and which end can be changed cheaply.
{% /concept-card %}

{% concept-card title="Associative" eyebrow="Key" icon="key" tone="violet" %}
Hash tables associate a key with a value. They optimize exact lookup but do not preserve sorted key order.
{% /concept-card %}

{% concept-card title="Ordered and priority" eyebrow="Comparison" icon="git-branch" tone="green" %}
Balanced trees, heaps, and tries preserve sorted order, an extreme value, or a shared key prefix.
{% /concept-card %}

{% concept-card title="Relationship and filter" eyebrow="Specialized" icon="network" tone="amber" %}
Graphs model arbitrary relationships. Bloom filters answer a narrower approximate-membership question with very little memory.
{% /concept-card %}
{% /concept-grid %}
{% /section-card %}

{% section-card %}
## Use the reference by decision boundary

Open a structure after identifying the operation you need to protect. Each entry names the useful operation profile and the condition that should make you reconsider it.

{% accordion defaultOpen="array" %}
{% accordion-item id="array" title="Array or dynamic array" %}

An array stores values contiguously and addresses a value by its numeric position. Dynamic arrays reserve spare capacity and occasionally allocate a larger region.

- **Critical path:** Indexed reads are O(1); iteration has strong cache locality.
- **Updates:** Append is amortized O(1). Inserting or deleting in the middle is O(n) because later values move.
- **System uses:** Batches, time-series chunks, image buffers, lookup tables, and the storage beneath many heaps.
- **Boundary:** Use a hash table for key lookup, or a linked representation when stable nodes and frequent known-position splices matter more than locality.

{% /accordion-item %}

{% accordion-item id="linked-list" title="Linked list" %}

A linked list stores each value in a separate node with a link to the next node and, in a doubly linked list, the previous node.

- **Critical path:** Insert or remove a known node in O(1); access by position and search are O(n).
- **Memory behavior:** Every node carries links and allocator metadata, and pointer chasing usually has poor cache locality.
- **System uses:** The recency order in an LRU cache, intrusive kernel lists, and queues whose nodes must remain stable.
- **Boundary:** Do not choose it merely because insertions are frequent. Finding the insertion point can still cost O(n), and dynamic arrays often win for compact sequential data.

{% /accordion-item %}

{% accordion-item id="stack" title="Stack" %}

A stack exposes one end of a sequence and removes the most recently added item first: last in, first out.

- **Critical path:** `push`, `pop`, and `peek` are O(1), amortized when a dynamic array grows.
- **Semantics:** Only the top is directly accessible; arbitrary search remains O(n).
- **System uses:** Function calls, parser state, depth-first search, backtracking, and undo history.
- **Boundary:** Bound growth or spill safely. An unbounded call or work stack can exhaust memory even when each operation is cheap.

{% /accordion-item %}

{% accordion-item id="queue" title="Queue and deque" %}

A queue removes the oldest item first: first in, first out. A deque supports insertion and removal at both ends.

- **Critical path:** A circular buffer or deque provides O(1) enqueue and dequeue without shifting all remaining values.
- **Semantics:** FIFO preserves arrival order; it does not provide arbitrary priority.
- **System uses:** Breadth-first search, ready-work buffers, producer-consumer handoff, and bounded in-process pipelines.
- **Boundary:** A distributed message broker adds durability, delivery, and coordination semantics that an in-memory queue alone does not provide.

{% /accordion-item %}

{% accordion-item id="balanced-tree" title="Balanced search tree" %}

A balanced search tree keeps comparable keys ordered while constraining its height. Common variants include AVL and red-black trees; databases often use wider B-trees or B+ trees for page-oriented storage.

- **Critical path:** Search, insertion, and deletion are O(log n); a range scan returning `k` items is O(log n + k).
- **Semantics:** Supports sorted iteration, nearest-key queries, minimum and maximum, and range boundaries.
- **System uses:** In-memory indexes, ordered maps, schedulers requiring both updates and ranges, and storage-engine indexes.
- **Boundary:** Nodes, comparisons, and balancing cost more than an expected O(1) hash lookup when order is unnecessary.

{% /accordion-item %}

{% accordion-item id="heap" title="Binary heap" %}

A binary heap is a complete tree, usually stored in an array, whose root is always the minimum or maximum value.

- **Critical path:** Read the minimum or maximum in O(1); insert and remove it in O(log n).
- **Semantics:** The root is ordered relative to descendants, but the full collection is not globally sorted.
- **System uses:** Priority queues, timer wheels with a heap tier, top-k selection, shortest-path algorithms, and job scheduling.
- **Boundary:** Arbitrary lookup is O(n). Pair the heap with a map when jobs also need direct identity-based updates.

{% /accordion-item %}

{% accordion-item id="trie" title="Trie or prefix tree" %}

A trie stores one key segment per edge so keys with common prefixes share a path. Compressed tries collapse single-child paths to reduce overhead.

- **Critical path:** Lookup, insertion, and prefix navigation are O(m) for a key of length `m`.
- **Semantics:** Prefixes are first-class; completion can enumerate descendants below a matched prefix.
- **System uses:** Autocomplete, dictionaries, routing prefixes, command dispatch, and lexicographic key navigation.
- **Boundary:** Sparse child maps can consume far more memory than the key data. Compression and alphabet representation are design decisions, not details.

{% /accordion-item %}

{% accordion-item id="hash-table" title="Hash table" %}

A hash table transforms a key into a bucket and resolves collisions with chaining or open addressing.

- **Critical path:** Lookup, insertion, and deletion are expected O(1) with a suitable hash function and controlled load factor.
- **Worst case:** Poor or adversarial collisions can degrade an operation to O(n); resizing can create latency spikes.
- **System uses:** Caches, session maps, deduplication sets, counters, symbol tables, and the identity index in composite structures.
- **Boundary:** Hash tables do not naturally support sorted iteration or range scans and commonly reserve extra capacity for speed.

{% /accordion-item %}

{% accordion-item id="graph" title="Graph" %}

A graph represents entities as vertices and relationships as edges. Direction and edge weight express different system semantics.

- **Critical path:** With an adjacency list, neighbor access is O(degree) and breadth-first or depth-first traversal is O(V + E).
- **Representation:** Adjacency lists use O(V + E) space; an adjacency matrix uses O(V^2) but provides O(1) edge checks.
- **System uses:** Social connections, dependency analysis, network routes, authorization relationships, and recommendation candidates.
- **Boundary:** Choose the traversal and storage from graph density and query shape. "Use a graph" does not select an algorithm or database.

{% /accordion-item %}

{% accordion-item id="bloom-filter" title="Bloom filter" %}

A Bloom filter sets `k` bit positions for each key. If any tested bit is zero, the key is definitely absent; if all are one, the key may be present.

- **Critical path:** Insert and membership test are O(k), where `k` is the configured number of hash probes.
- **Semantics:** Standard Bloom filters have false positives but no false negatives while bits are only added and the same hashing scheme is used.
- **System uses:** Avoiding unnecessary storage reads, crawler deduplication pre-checks, cache admission, and database filtering.
- **Boundary:** It cannot retrieve values or prove presence. Standard filters cannot safely delete arbitrary keys; counting variants spend more memory to support deletion.

{% /accordion-item %}
{% /accordion %}
{% /section-card %}

{% section-card accent="amber" %}
## Compose structures around the full workload

Production components often combine representations because one structure rarely makes every required operation cheap.

{% concept-grid columns="3" %}
{% concept-card title="LRU cache" eyebrow="Hash table + linked list" icon="cache" tone="blue" %}
The hash table finds a cache entry by key in expected O(1). A doubly linked list moves that known entry to the most-recent position and evicts the oldest in O(1).
{% /concept-card %}

{% concept-card title="Mutable priority queue" eyebrow="Heap + identity map" icon="gauge" tone="violet" %}
The heap selects the next job. A map finds a job's heap position when its priority changes or it must be cancelled.
{% /concept-card %}

{% concept-card title="Filtered exact store" eyebrow="Bloom filter + database" icon="database" tone="green" %}
The filter rejects definite misses in memory. Possible matches continue to the exact database, preserving correctness while reducing expensive reads.
{% /concept-card %}
{% /concept-grid %}
{% /section-card %}

{% section-card %}
## Validate the production choice

Before committing to an implementation:

1. Record operation frequencies, latency targets, peak item count, and value sizes.
2. Verify semantic requirements such as exactness, ordering, duplicates, stable references, and concurrency.
3. Model both expected and worst-case behavior, including resize, rebalance, collision, and skew paths.
4. Measure total memory, not only payload bytes: include capacity, links, buckets, allocator metadata, and garbage-collector cost.
5. Benchmark representative distributions and access patterns at target scale.
6. Define the signal that would trigger a different structure or a composite design.

{% callout variant="tip" %}
Choose the simplest structure that meets the measured contract. An advanced representation without a workload reason increases implementation and operational risk without buying useful performance.
{% /callout %}
{% /section-card %}

